PART 1 — UNDERSTAND THE PRODUCT

1. What FramePilot is supposed to do

FramePilot is a visual authoring and animation-direction application.

Its primary purpose is to let me:

1. Draw characters, objects, environments, backgrounds, props, shapes and visual compositions.
2. Create rough sketches, including extremely simple stick figures.
3. Organize drawings using layers.
4. Create frame-by-frame animation.
5. Direct the movement of characters and individual objects.
6. Create complex motion graphics, including paths, shape transformations, kinetic typography, particles and camera movements.
7. Describe everything in natural language so AI models can understand my intentions.
8. Export a complete, structured production package that text-only AI coding models can understand without relying on image or vision capabilities.

The application is not primarily intended to render the finished video.

It should make it easy for me to communicate exactly what I imagine, then provide OpenCode with enough structured information to implement the actual animation.

2. My intended users and experience

Design this for someone who:

- Is not a professional animator.
- May not understand traditional animation terminology.
- Wants to draw freely and experiment.
- May only know how to draw basic shapes or stick figures.
- Wants to create Instagram Reels, Shorts, motion graphics and animated scenes.
- Wants creative freedom without having to learn complicated software first.
- Uses AI coding models to implement the final animation.
- Needs those models to understand visual instructions even when they cannot interpret images.

I should not need to understand skeleton rigs, interpolation, easing curves, coordinate systems or keyframe terminology before I can create my first animation.

Introduce these concepts gradually through simple actions.


